    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nA part of my workflow had always been exiting out of a file to get to its neighboring files, even though I use a picker sometimes that's faster for me. Recently (on nightly) I found both on windows and linux that the `:Ex` command doesn't go to the file's current directory but to neovim's `:pwd`. I can't tell you exactly what version of nightly this broke at, since I've been away from my PCs for a bit and just blanket updated them all.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nGo to a file nested inside some dirs, `:Ex`, you're now on netrw in the dir you entered nvim in, happens also on `nvim --clean`\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nShould open netrw in the directory of the file\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-970+g41294e4253\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nvim 8.2.280 goes to file's wd\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nwindows 11, arch, fedora 44\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nwezterm nightly\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nscoop on windows, aur on arch, copr on fedora",
